  "title": "The flitter: one animal working the flowers",
  "question": "Does this read as an animal that lives on flowers -- and is the hop the one you liked?",
  "context_md": "**The first animal in the box whose living is meant to be flowers.** Nectar, the bloom sense and re-bloom all landed on `main` and were all invisible, because nothing could reach a flower: a ground animal with eyes and a pull tops out one row under it. The flitter is two cells, pale, hops on purpose, and a flower is worth four times as much to it as to an ant.\n\n**Hand-authored, on your ruling** -- nothing evolved it. It carries no pollen yet; that is the next brief.\n\n**What is already measured, so you need not judge it here.** It reaches the canopy: highest head **86 rows** above the soil against an ant's **12** and the hopper's **18**, so the reach gap that stopped the hopper is closed. It breeds: **26-40 generations deep** over 120,000 frames where the hopper reaches **1**. And it does not yet live on flowers -- over 120,000 frames it drinks at one **16 times** on the best seed and **not once** on another, while eating ordinary leaf all day.\n\n**The middle and right strips are the same bed and the same seed at the same frame, with and without them**, and that pair is what I am least sure about: the tree and the shrub on the right are eaten back, plant cells **960 -> 566**, flowers standing **34 -> 4**, and there are **452** flitters. The design predicted this and called it graded rather than defective. Does it read as a pollinator or as a plague?\n\n**One thing that is not the animal's fault:** the lab opens on ANIMALS WEAR BY COLONY, so everything is drawn in a group colour and the pale blue-white body this species authors never appears unless you cycle to OWN COLOUR. Ants and flitters are both blue-ish in these strips for that reason.",
